From: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 19:45:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] net: dsa: realtek: rtl8365mb: let the host's multicast out
 under the 802.1Q tagger

With FDB isolation and MDB support the DSA core turns the multicast list
of a standalone port, and of a bridge, into host MDB entries: L2 entries
whose only member is the CPU port. The Realtek tag of rtl8_4 forces the
egress port of a frame from the host, so those entries never meet the
host's own frames. The 802.1Q tagger forces nothing: the switch looks
the DA of a frame from the host up like any other, finds the entry of a
group the host is in and, the CPU being the source port, drops the
frame.

On a TP-Link Archer AX55 that took out ff02::1 and ff02::2 on the WAN
port, and with them the router solicitations odhcpd relays upstream, and
the neighbour solicitations for every address whose solicited-node group
the host had joined too (the gateway's ::1, the addresses relayed by
NDP proxy). Groups the host is not in were flooded and went out.

Give an entry of the host the user ports of its database as members as
well: the standalone port, or the ports of the bridge. Traffic arriving
on those ports still goes to the CPU, and the host's frames leave
through the ports they were sent to. Deleting the entry of the host
clears those ports again, which also clears them from a snooped entry
of one of those ports for the same group and VID; the bridge puts them
back with the next report.

Signed-off-by: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/dsa/realtek/rtl8365mb_main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/dsa/realtek/rtl8365mb_main.c
@@ -4181,15 +4181,59 @@
 				    rtl8365mb_db_vid(ds->priv, db, vid), db);
 }
 
+/* Under the 802.1Q tagger nothing in a frame from the host steers it to a
+ * port: the switch looks its DA up like that of any other frame. A host MDB
+ * entry, which only has the CPU port as member, then catches the host's own
+ * multicast to that group - ff02::1, ff02::2, the solicited-node groups of
+ * its addresses - and the frame dies on its source port. So an entry of the
+ * host also gets the user ports of its database as members: the standalone
+ * port, or the ports of the bridge. Traffic coming in on those ports still
+ * reaches the CPU, and the host's frames leave where they are meant to.
+ */
+static int rtl8365mb_port_mdb_host_ports(struct dsa_switch *ds, int port,
+					 const struct switchdev_obj_port_mdb *m,
+					 struct dsa_db db, bool add)
+{
+	struct dsa_port *dp;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (!rtl8365mb_is_8021q(ds->priv) || !dsa_is_cpu_port(ds, port))
+		return 0;
+
+	dsa_switch_for_each_user_port(dp, ds) {
+		if (db.type == DSA_DB_PORT && dp != db.dp)
+			continue;
+		if (db.type == DSA_DB_BRIDGE &&
+		    !dsa_port_offloads_bridge_dev(dp, db.bridge.dev))
+			continue;
+		if (db.type != DSA_DB_PORT && db.type != DSA_DB_BRIDGE)
+			continue;
+
+		if (add)
+			ret = rtl83xx_port_mdb_add(ds, dp->index, m, db);
+		else
+			ret = rtl83xx_port_mdb_del(ds, dp->index, m, db);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+	}
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
 static int rtl8365mb_port_mdb_add(struct dsa_switch *ds, int port,
 				  const struct switchdev_obj_port_mdb *mdb,
 				  struct dsa_db db)
 {
 	struct switchdev_obj_port_mdb m = *mdb;
+	int ret;
 
 	m.vid = rtl8365mb_db_vid(ds->priv, db, mdb->vid);
 
-	return rtl83xx_port_mdb_add(ds, port, &m, db);
+	ret = rtl83xx_port_mdb_add(ds, port, &m, db);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	return rtl8365mb_port_mdb_host_ports(ds, port, &m, db, true);
 }
 
 static int rtl8365mb_port_mdb_del(struct dsa_switch *ds, int port,
@@ -4197,9 +4241,14 @@
 				  struct dsa_db db)
 {
 	struct switchdev_obj_port_mdb m = *mdb;
+	int ret;
 
 	m.vid = rtl8365mb_db_vid(ds->priv, db, mdb->vid);
 
+	ret = rtl8365mb_port_mdb_host_ports(ds, port, &m, db, false);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
 	return rtl83xx_port_mdb_del(ds, port, &m, db);
 }
 
